Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Building Tensor Networks", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"69c92734-a80d-4b66-9271-aa11ac01cb9a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
This tutorial covers the basics of building tensor networks in the Wolfram \
Language. It begins with the TensorNetwork object itself \[LongDash] what it \
bundles, how to interrogate it, and what makes the head a distinct value \
rather than an opaque tagged list. It then walks through the four constructor \
patterns: tensors plus hyperedges, einsum-style rules with an explicit output \
specification, conversion from an Inactive TensorContract expression, and a \
symbolic-skeleton constructor that builds ArraySymbol placeholders from a \
hypergraph and dimension association alone. The named-topology section uses \
RandomTensorNetwork to generate instances of the standard families \
\[LongDash] matrix product states and operators, tensor trains, PEPS on a 2D \
grid, tree tensor networks, and MERA \[LongDash] including the boundary and \
field options shared by the chain families. Property dispatch covers the \
tn[\"...\"] interface and the underlying TensorNetworkData association. \
Hyperedges and binarization explains the SymbolicDeltaProductArray spider \
trick that lets pairwise contraction-path algorithms consume otherwise \
non-binary networks. Edits and transformations cover TensorNetworkAdd, \
TensorNetworkDelete, TensorNetworkReplaceIndices on the graph form, \
SparseTensorNetwork, and ToTensorNetworkGraph.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fc53afa1-91c8-49dd-a3eb-2c6a2ce7e740"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<Wolfram`TensorNetworks`\>\"", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[59]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c44db1b1-0ba7-47e3-aef3-02b51eae0adb"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1. The TensorNetwork object", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9eaa5f9d-c5db-4767-8699-d38c7106f402"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A tensor network is a collection of tensors wired together by named legs. \
The fundamental Wolfram-Language container for one is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetwork",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetwork"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"08377f31-1c69-412e-b373-4cec58f3b65f"],
 ", with the canonical signature ",
 StyleBox["TensorNetwork[tensors, hyperedges, output]", "Code"],
 ". The three pieces of state are a flat list of numeric (or symbolic) \
tensors, a list of hyperedges \[LongDash] each itself a list of integer leg \
labels naming the indices that the corresponding tensor exposes \[LongDash] \
and an output specification controlling which legs survive as free indices \
after the network is contracted. The output argument is optional; it defaults \
to ",
 StyleBox["Automatic", "Code"],
 ", which keeps exactly the indices that appear in only one tensor (the \
network's open legs). Any index that appears more than once is treated as \
contracted."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"14ea275b-a426-4714-bfec-2ed8105c68ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Conceptually the hyperedges play the role of a hypergraph: each integer \
label is a vertex of the hypergraph; each inner list is an edge naming the \
legs of one tensor. The network does not commit to a contraction order. Path \
search, binarization, and evaluation are all deferred to downstream functions \
that consume the object \[LongDash] see ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetworkContract",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetworkContract"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"bb8c8c9c-0a69-4758-a3bf-8e61f3fec018"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OptimalContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/OptimalContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"f7cb0729-623a-4219-9b81-21784ef1bee9"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"382aef85-07e7-4629-bfe6-7ad670a730cd"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Prepare two example tensors.", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dc70ecab-f3a0-4cae-8345-7b60e6532387"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"A", "=", 
   RowBox[{"RandomReal", "[", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";", " ", 
  RowBox[{"B", "=", 
   RowBox[{"RandomReal", "[", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.988058759722665*^9, 3.98805875991085*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[60]:=",ExpressionUUID->"489b41c9-4ef3-4447-aa7f-995d672114a3"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Wrap them in a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetwork",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetwork"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"20862a17-664a-4a01-99c3-c3fcfb142c7f"],
 " by passing the tensor list and a hyperedge list. The matrices ",
 StyleBox["A", "Code"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["B", "Code"],
 " each have rank 2, so each inner hyperedge list has length 2. The shared \
label ",
 StyleBox["2", "Code"],
 " names the joining bond \[LongDash] it appears in both hyperedges and will \
be contracted away. Labels ",
 StyleBox["1", "Code"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["3", "Code"],
 " each appear exactly once and remain free."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"759ab539-cd14-40ad-b863-894012867958"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"tn", "=", 
  RowBox[{"TensorNetwork", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"A", ",", "B"}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[61]:=",ExpressionUUID->"ea2d03c6-d3c8-4d10-9cc5-c6b83374e091"],

